'''aglee, agley''' (a̤-glē′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' [< ''a-''<sup>3</sup> + Sc. ''gley'', ''gleg'', squint, oblique look: see ''gley''.] Off the right line; obliquely; wrong. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|<poem>The best laid schemes o’ mice an’ men
Gang aft ''a-gley.''</poem>
{{right|''Burns'', To a Mouse.}}}}

'''aglet, aiglet''' (ag′let, āg′let), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''agglet'', < ME. ''aglet'', ''aglette'', < OF. ''aguillette'', ''aiguillette'', F. ''aiguillette'', a point, dim. of ''aiguille'', < ML. ''acucula'', dim. of L. ''acus'', a needle: see ''acus''.] '''1.''' A tag or metal sheathing of the end of a lace, or of the points (see ''point'') or ribbons generally used in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries to fasten or tie dresses. {{smaller|They were originally intended simply to facilitate the passing of the ends through the eyelet-holes, as in modern shoe-laces and stay-laces, but were afterward frequently formed of the precious metals, carved into small figures, and suspended from the ribbon, etc., as ornaments (whence Shakspere’s phrase “an aglet-baby,” which see); and they are still so used in the form of tagged points or braid hanging from the shoulder in some military uniforms, now officially styled ''aiguillettes''. Also written ''aigulet''.}}
{{smaller block|And on his head an hood with ''aglets'' sprad.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. ii. 5.}}
His gown, addressed with ''aglets'', esteemed worth 25''l''.
{{right|''Sir J. Hayward'', Life of Edw. VI.}}}}
'''2†.''' In ''bot.'', a pendent anther; also, a loose pendent catkin, as of the birch.

'''aglet-baby†''' (ag′let-bā″bi), ''n.'' A small image on the end of a lace. See ''aglet''.
{{smaller block|Marry him to a puppet, or an ''aglet-baby''.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., i. 2.}}}}

'''agley''', ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' See ''aglee''.

